WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:06.460
"Andor" may focus on the Rebellion's unsung
hero, but this galaxy might not be as far,

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:10.304
far away from the Star Wars mainstream as
fans expect.

00:00:10.539 --> 00:00:16.359
In the very first episode of "Andor," viewers
see flashbacks to Cassian's childhood on Kenari

00:00:16.359 --> 00:00:17.590
his home planet.

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:22.320
The clips show him donning combat gear and
weapons to investigate a crashed ship with

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:27.429
the other inhabitants of his village, a mission
that ultimately proves life-changing, and

00:00:27.429 --> 00:00:29.599
not necessarily in the best way.

00:00:29.599 --> 00:00:33.700
It's interesting to see such a young version
of Cassian running around with a spear in

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:37.910
his hand, but if you've seen "Rogue One,"
you may have been expecting it.

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:42.688
In the movie, Cassian tells Jyn Erso about
his long history as a freedom fighter.

00:00:42.688 --> 00:00:46.090
"I've been in this fight since I was six years
old."

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:50.760
It emphasizes just how much of his life has
been spent either on the run or in direct

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:53.050
conflict with the powers that be.

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:58.350
While the ship he's investigating isn't Imperial,
"Andor" does make good on Cassian's claim

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:01.631
of being a part of the good fight from a very
young age.

00:01:01.631 --> 00:01:06.600
Though the "Star Wars" franchise has featured
a ton of different starships over the decades,

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:11.990
some have been iconic enough to stand out
anywhere, even in a random shipyard on Ferrix

00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:14.170
where Cassian is currently living.

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:18.900
Near the end of "Andor" Season 1's opening
episode, Cassian rummages around in an old

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:21.810
junker that he borrowed from one of the yard's
employees.

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:27.530
He plays it cool, but he's actually scrubbing
incriminating info from the ship's records.

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:32.260
During a couple of exterior shots of the ship,
viewers may recognize a few of the other ships

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:33.850
occupying the yard.

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:39.640
One is unmistakably a Y-Wing starfighter,
a bomber commonly used by the Rebel Alliance

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.780
and first seen during the run on the original
Death Star.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:48.600
The faded yellow cockpit of the ship can be
seen clearly, complete with the iconic rotating turret.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:53.590
Y-Wings were first used by the Republic during
the Clone Wars, so it makes sense to see a

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:56.880
stray one junking it up on a planet like Ferrix.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:03.000
The scene also shows what looks like a variant
of the T-47 airspeeder from "The Empire Strikes Back."

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:08.929
Originally manufactured as stunt fighters,
T-47s were modified for various purposes by

00:02:08.929 --> 00:02:11.730
rebels throughout the Galactic Civil War.

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:14.780
They became known as snowspeeders among the
rebels.

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:20.290
The ship seen opposite the Y-Wing in the "Andor"
shipyard isn't a snowspeeder, it's much longer

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:25.657
in the back than a normal T-47, but the cockpit
and the wing design are quite evocative.

00:02:25.767 --> 00:02:31.260
One of the most distinct aspects of the "Star
Wars" universe is its retro sci-fi tech aesthetic.

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:36.350
When the first film was released in 1977,
computers used vector graphics.

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:41.920
As the franchise continued and real-world
technology evolved, "Star Wars" played with

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:46.670
different variants on its original in-universe
technology, like how the prequels employed

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:49.400
more holographic screens instead of vectors.

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:54.470
"Andor" makes it clear from the get-go that
its inspiration comes fully from the original,

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:57.370
old-school tech of the first "Star Wars" films.

00:02:57.370 --> 00:03:01.690
From the numerous computer screens filled
with hard lines to the digital readouts on

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:06.940
Luthen Rael's binoculars, the show fully embraces
the retro-futuristic look.

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:12.610
It's similar in many ways to the aesthetic
of "The Mandalorian," but with an early Imperial era twist.

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:17.370
If you keep your eyes peeled throughout "Andor"
Season 1, you'll catch a lot of little gadgets

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:20.865
and gizmos that have appeared in previous
"Star Wars" stories.

00:03:20.865 --> 00:03:25.709
In Cassian's flashbacks to the ship crash
on his home world of Kenari, we see how he

00:03:25.709 --> 00:03:30.620
came to be raised by Maarva Andor and taken
far away into the galaxy.

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:35.040
It all centers around the shipwreck itself,
which Cassian investigates with a party from

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:36.290
his village.

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:41.090
Things go sour when some of the passengers
turn out to be both alive and hostile, leading

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:43.040
to a violent confrontation.

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:46.349
Who are these interlopers, and where were
they headed?

00:03:46.349 --> 00:03:51.770
It's not entirely clear, but the familiar
logo on their uniforms gives some indication.

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:56.340
On the sleeves of their spacesuits, you can
make out the segmented blue and white hexagon

00:03:56.340 --> 00:04:00.430
that was the symbol of the Separatist Alliance
during the Clone Wars.

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:04.680
There are no battle droids to be seen aboard
the ship, but diehard fans will know that

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:09.599
plenty of non-robotic beings gave their lives
to the Separatist cause as well.

00:04:09.599 --> 00:04:14.640
This also might explain why the wounded crewman
immediately gets violent after waking up.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:19.750
The ship may have been coming directly from
a warzone, though of course, that's no justification

00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:21.000
for his actions.

00:04:21.147 --> 00:04:26.311
When the corporate security agents come to
Ferrix to hunt Cassian in Episode 3, they

00:04:26.311 --> 00:04:27.820
come prepared.

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:32.290
Not only do they gear up with some major firepower,
but they drop down to the planet's surface

00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:37.740
in ships that are strikingly reminiscent of
the Republic gunships used in the Clone Wars.

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:42.190
The iconic design was first seen in "Attack
of the Clones," and it has remained a staple

00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:43.920
of "Star Wars" ever since.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:47.940
While there are certainly differences between
the old gunships and the landing craft seen

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:52.540
in "Andor," their striking similarities are
interesting, to say the least.

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:57.924
In the wake of the Clone Wars, the Empire
phases out a lot of the gear used by the clone army.

00:04:57.924 --> 00:05:05.020
Old starfighters become TIE Fighters, AT-TEs
become AT-ATs, and the old clone gunships

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:06.740
are nowhere to be found.

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:11.290
It would make sense for a private military
company like the one seen in "Andor" to purchase

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:16.360
some of this surplus tech for its own purposes,
perhaps even making some modifications along

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:17.370
the way.

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:22.300
That could explain the ship design in Episode
3, or maybe the same manufacturer simply built

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:24.335
some new models after the war.

00:05:24.335 --> 00:05:29.710
During their first meeting in "Andor" Episode
3, Luthen Rael asks how Cassian managed to

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:33.390
steal the "untraceable" Imperial tech he's
bartering off.

00:05:33.390 --> 00:05:35.301
"You just walk in like you belong.

00:05:35.301 --> 00:05:38.620
To steal from the Empire, what do you need?

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:41.842
A uniform, some dirty hands, and an Imperial
toolkit."

00:05:41.842 --> 00:05:47.009
His explanation can be taken as a nod to the
many, many times rebels have successfully

00:05:47.009 --> 00:05:51.570
infiltrated Imperial strongholds using basic
disguises throughout the franchise.

00:05:51.570 --> 00:05:55.500
It's a long and storied trope worthy of the
reference.

00:05:55.500 --> 00:06:00.500
Whether it's Han and Luke disguising themselves
as Stormtroopers on the Death Star or Obi-Wan

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:06.350
Kenobi sneaking around the Imperial Inquisitor's
citadel escorted by a double agent, many characters

00:06:06.350 --> 00:06:12.510
have walked right past the Empire's defenses
with little more than courage and a cool hat.

00:06:12.510 --> 00:06:17.770
Cassian himself uses this exact tactic at
the end of "Rogue One," when he and Jyn Erso

00:06:17.770 --> 00:06:22.350
break into the Imperial base on Scarif to
steal the Death Star plans.

00:06:22.350 --> 00:06:27.770
As such, his line about "borrowing" uniforms
can also be taken as a grim foreshadowing

00:06:27.770 --> 00:06:29.322
of his eventual demise.

00:06:29.410 --> 00:06:35.220
At the end of "Andor" Episode 3, we're treated
to a beautifully shot sequence that parallels

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:40.948
Cassian's departure from Kenari as a young
boy with his escape from Ferrix with Luthen Rael.

00:06:40.948 --> 00:06:45.039
It's a powerful and haunting moment, showing
the character's talent for making it out by

00:06:45.039 --> 00:06:46.479
the skin of his teeth.

00:06:46.479 --> 00:06:52.100
The scene is tragic, not only because it emphasizes
how Cassian must repeatedly leave things behind,

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:56.310
but because it foreshadows the one time he
finally doesn't make it out.

00:06:56.310 --> 00:07:00.250
Most people who watch "Andor" will already
know Cassian's ultimate fate.

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:04.610
It's particularly affecting, then, to see
that earlier in his life, Cassian was known

00:07:04.610 --> 00:07:06.440
for narrow getaways.

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:11.150
So many different times, he almost meets his
end, only to narrowly escape.

00:07:11.150 --> 00:07:16.462
His wits take him far in his struggle against
totalitarianism, but even Cassian Andor's

00:07:16.462 --> 00:07:18.692
luck must eventually run out.

